327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (Mexico reformed its labor-justice system between 2019 and 2023. Disputes no longer start in the old Conciliation and Arbitration Boards. Instead:)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1565 0 R
/E (The CFCRL \(Federal Center for Conciliation and Labor Registration\) runs a mandatory, out-of-court conciliation stage that most disputes must pass through before any lawsuit can be filed.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1566 0 R
/E (Labor Courts \(Tribunales Laborales\), part of the judicial branch, hear cases only if conciliation fails.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 206 0 R
/E (A large majority of disputes are now settled at the conciliation stage rather than in court. This makes early, well-documented, HR-led handling of any issue far more important than litigation strategy.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [13]
>>
